# Auto-compiled from learning outcomes.xlsx supplied by the user.
LEARNING_OUTCOMES = {'Sec 1 G3': [{'row': 4, 'text': 'Highlight that the prime factorisation of a number (including index notation) is an equivalent form which allows us to study the characteristics of that number. [big ideas about equivalence and notation] Activity 1 Provide mathematical statements where students need to reason and justify by applying their understanding of factors, multiples and prime factorisation. [applying, communication and reasoning] Activity 1'}, {'row': 6, 'text': 'Highlight that, given any sequence of numbers (applies to all real numbers, including negatives, and later extending to algebra), re-arranging the sequence does not change its sum or product. [big ideas about invariance] Activity 1 Extend understanding with the number line to real numbers (including negatives), appreciate it as a representation that allows us to order and compare numbers, and understand that there are infinitely many values between two points on the number line. [big ideas about diagrams] Activity 2'}, {'row': 8, 'text': 'Use real-world contexts to help students appreciate the use of significant figures and how rounding off to different degrees of accuracy can have an impact on subsequent computation. [applying, appreciation] Activity 3'}, {'row': 9, 'text': '1. Verbalise the structures in algebraic expressions and the manipulations involved in simplifying them. This can be done using algebra discs to help students visualise. [communicating] Activity 1 Part A 2. Abstract the general properties in algebraic manipulations. [big ideas about equivalence] Activity 1 Part B 3. Create and manipulate their own algebraic expressions, and then to reflect on their approaches [interest, reasoning and metacognition] Activity 2'}, {'row': 11, 'text': '1. Verbalise the structures in algebraic expressions and the manipulations involved in simplifying them. This can be done using algebra discs to help students visualise. [communicating] Activity 1 Part A 2. Abstract the general properties in algebraic manipulations. [big ideas about equivalence] Activity 1 Part B 3. Create and manipulate their own algebraic expressions, and then to reflect on their approaches [interest, reasoning and metacognition] Activity 2 Role-model and then get students to verbalise the structures in algebraic expressions and the manipulations involved in simplifying them. This can be done using algebra discs to help students visualise. [communicating] Activity 1 Part A 2. Help students to abstract the general properties in algebraic manipulations. [big ideas about equivalence] Activity 1 Part B'}, {'row': 13, 'text': '1. Provide examples and explain using the notion of variable and unknown to help students distinguish the difference between algebraic expressions and equations [concept of equation] Activity 1 2. Highlight the interpretation of the equal sign in an equation, and the meaning of “solving an equation”, “solution of an equation” and “equivalent equations” [concept of equation] Activity 1 3. Use the metaphor of algebra discs on a balance to help students understand that the algorithm of solving a linear equation is premised on the idea of equivalence [big idea of equivalence] Activity 2 4. Compare and critique different methods of solving an equation and reflect on one’s own approach and answer [reasoning and metacognition] Activity 3, Question 1 5. Appreciate and ease into the formulation of linear equations and use the algebraic method to solve word problems [transition from the model method] Activity 4'}, {'row': 17, 'text': '1.Build the idea of functions by using an input-rule-output relationship and guide them to recognise that the input-rule-output relationship can be represented by pairs of coordinate points (input (x), output (y)) on the Cartesian plane. (big ideas about functions and notations) Activity 1 2. Deepen understanding of linear functions and appreciate their use for modelling real-world contexts. (applying, big ideas about functions and models) Activity 2'}, {'row': 19, 'text': 'Use geometric patterns to discuss how we can describe and formulate the general term of a sequence in different ways and hence leverage the opportunity to talk about equivalence expressions. [reasoning, big ideas about equivalence] Activity 3'}, {'row': 20, 'text': '1. Highlight the big ideas about proportionality in percentages, i.e. if x% is related to y, 1% is related to y/x, the constant of proportionality, and y/x can be used to find other percentages. [big ideas about proportionality] 2. Highlight that a number can be represented as a fraction, decimal and percentage with the same value, and that we use the equivalent form which is best for analysis, interpretation or comparison, depending on the context. [big ideas about equivalence] 3. Provide opportunities to reason and communicate when using percentages for comparison and when discussing percentage increase or decrease relative to the initial values. To distinguish between absolute comparison and relative comparison [reasoning and communicating; addressing misconception] Activity 1 & 2 4. Reflect on their strategies when solving problems involving percentages [applying and metacognition] Activity 3'}, {'row': 22, 'text': '1. Verbalise the relationship between two quantities using ratios [reasoning and communicating] Activity 1 2. Solve problems that allow students to distinguish between absolute comparison and relative comparison [addressing misconception] Activity 2 and generate equivalent ratios [big ideas about equivalence] Activity 3 3. Highlight the connections between ratios, fractions and decimals, e.g. given a : b = 1 : 4, we can also said that a is 1/4 of b or a is 1/5 of the whole (assuming as sum of a and b), or b is 0.8 of the whole [big ideas about equivalence] 4. Bring out the idea of proportionality and that ratio expresses proportional relationship between quantities and hence allow one quantity to be computed from the other based on multiplication reasoning [big ideas about proportionality] Activity 4 5. Solve problems involving ratios in different contexts and find ‘the point of comparison’, and evaluate and reflect on their approaches [applying, reasoning and metacognition] Activity 5 & 6'}, {'row': 23, 'text': '1. Highlight that a rate is a measure combining measures of two attributes and the unit used is derived from the units of the two measures. [big ideas about measures] Activity 1 2. Use meaningful and interesting contexts to get students to talk about the difference between speed and average speed. [interest/appreciation/motivation, reasoning and communicating] Activity 2 & 3 3. Bring out the idea of proportionality in different contexts involving (constant) rates and highlight that there is a constant (of proportionality) that relates the two attributes. [big ideas about proportionality] Activity 4 4. Use rates to solve problems, and reflect on their strategies [applying and metacognition] Activity 5'}, {'row': 24, 'text': '1. Highlight that an angle is a measure of the amount of rotation between two lines so that it can be analysed and compared. [big ideas about measures] Activity 1 2. Strengthen students’ spatial visualisation by exposing them to a variety of geometrical drawings in different orientations and hands-on experiences such as paper-folding. [visualising space] Activity 2 3. Help students establish the properties of geometrical objects through inductive or deductive reasoning. [big ideas about invariance] Activity 3'}, {'row': 27, 'text': '4. Develop students’ understanding and use of notations and their conventions to read and write statements for concise and meaningful communication of mathematical ideas, including logical mathematical arguments. [big ideas about notations] 5. Highlight to students how the features of geometrical diagrams help them interpret the information presented about the relationships between points, lines and angles in plane geometry. [big ideas about diagrams] Activity 4 6. Encourage students to explore using different approaches and form different lines of argument to solve the same problem. [reasoning and communicating, metacognition] Activity 5 7. Make link to real-world contexts as motivation for the learning of geometry and to highlight its numerous applications to many aspects of our life. [appreciation and motivation] Activity 6'}, {'row': 29, 'text': '1. Highlight that area and perimeter are measures of the space and boundary of 2D shapes while volume and surface area are measures of the space and boundary of 3D solids. [big ideas about measures] Activity 1 2. Create opportunities for students to use what they have learnt in mensuration to solve problems in which real-world objects are modelled using basic shapes and solids [big ideas about models, appreciation, motivation] Activity 1'}, {'row': 31, 'text': '3. Get students to observe and talk about the connections between the formulae for finding area and volume of related shapes and slides. [reasoning and communicating] Activity 2 4. Guide students to realise that composing and decomposing 2D shapes and 3D solids conserves their area and volume. [visualising space] Activity 2 5. Strengthen students’ spatial visualisation by asking them to manipulate, examine and sketch the different perspectives of real-world objects. [big ideas about diagrams, visualising space, appreciation, motivation] Activity 3'}, {'row': 33, 'text': '1. Pose and solve problems that require collecting, organising, representing, analysing and interpreting data. [applying, communicating, metacognition, appreciation and motivation] Activity 1 2. Highlight the importance of proportionality in statistical diagrams [big ideas about proportionality, big Ideas about diagrams] Activity 2'}, {'row': 35, 'text': 'strengthen students’ understanding of the features of dot diagrams, histograms and stem- and-leaf diagrams through opportunities to construct, interpret and discuss about these representations. [big ideas about diagrams, reasoning and communicating, metacognition] Activity 1'}], 'Sec 2 G3': [{'row': 42, 'text': '1.Build the idea of functions by using an input-rule-output relationship and guide them to recognise that the input-rule-output relationship can be represented by pairs of coordinate points (input (x), output (y)) on the Cartesian plane. (big ideas about functions and notations) Activity 1 2. Deepen understanding of linear functions and appreciate their use for modelling real-world contexts. (applying, big ideas about functions and models) Activity 2'}, {'row': 43, 'text': '1. Provide opportunities for students to formulate and solve simultaneous linear equations using a real-life context. [appreciation, applying] through Activity 1. 2. Help students make connections between the solution of a pair of simultaneous equations and their graphical representations. [big ideas about diagrams] when they are attempting Activity 1. 3. Develop students’ metacognition by getting them to compare methods and rationalise their choice of method when solving simultaneous linear equations, [metacognition] through Activity 2. 4. Highlight the power of the visual representations that graphs can provide to help students appreciate the usefulness of the graphical approach in solving simultaneous linear equations [big ideas about diagrams] from Activity 3.'}, {'row': 45, 'text': '1. Firstly, introduce to the students the concept of inequality and its notations using real life contexts. [appreciation, big ideas about notations] through Activity 1. 2. Get students to discuss the similarities and differences between equations and inequalities, and their notations and representations on a number line, [big ideas about notations and diagrams] through Activity 2. 3. Students are able to communicate, reason, and interpret mathematical ideas about inequalities using contextual problems. [interest, communicating and reasoning] through Activity 3.'}, {'row': 48, 'text': 'Explain the following processes, using algebra discs and the multiplication frame: i. expanding the product of two linear expressions of the form 𝑝𝑥 + 𝑞, where 𝑝 and 𝑞 are integers, to obtain a quadratic expression. ii. factorising quadratic expressions, highlighting expansion and factorisation as reverse processes of each other.'}, {'row': 50, 'text': 'To explain and apply the Special algebraic Identities to questions correctly.'}, {'row': 54, 'text': '1) Develop understanding of the properties of quadratic functions using the algebraic, tabular and graphical form, and appreciate big ideas about functions and diagrams. [big ideas about functions, and diagrams] 2) To appreciate the use of quadratic functions to model real-world phenomena and the use of models to communicate ideas and answer relevant questions. [appreciation, big ideas about models, and reasoning & communicating]'}, {'row': 58, 'text': '1) Help students appreciate the value of changing the subject of a formula using real-world contexts. 2) Make connections with solving equations when changing the subject of a formula to help students understand that by applying the same operation on both sides of the equation to maintain balance, the series of algebraic statements produced through the process of manipulation are equivalent to each other. [big ideas about equivalence] Go to SLS → Changing the Subject of a Formula →Video “Changing the Subject of a Formula” 3) Get students to compare and critique different methods of changing the subject of a formula and help students recognise that the final answer, expressed as an algebraic expression, may have other equivalent forms. 4) Activate students’ prior knowledge about simplifying numerical fractions and help students make sense of how and why an algebraic fraction remains equivalent throughout the process of simplification.'}, {'row': 61, 'text': '1) Discuss and interpret scales used in maps and floor plans, etc in order to visualise actual distances and areas. 2) Discuss examples of direct and inverse proportion and explain the concepts using tables, equations and graphs.'}, {'row': 64, 'text': '1) Get students to discuss ideas about similar figures or objects. [reasoning and communicating] 2) Provide opportunity for students to articulate the proportional relationship in a pair of similar figures and a family of similar figures. [big ideas about proportionality & notations, metacognition] 3) Create opportunity for students to appreciate the application of the concept of similarity in daily life. [applying]'}, {'row': 68, 'text': '1) students construct their understanding about Pythagoras’ Theorem, and consolidate and communicate mathematical ideas clearly using appropriate diagrams and notations. [communicating, reasoning, big ideas about diagrams and notations] 2) Students to discover through investigation that the Pythagoras’ Theorem and its converse are equivalent statements. [big ideas about equivalence] 3) Provide students with opportunity to make sense of the trigonometric notations and appreciate that trigonometric ratios reflect the proportional relationships between pairs of sides of right-angled triangles. [big ideas about notations and proportionality]'}, {'row': 69, 'text': 'Teachers should: 1. Help students construct their understanding about Pythagoras’ Theorem, and consolidate and communicate mathematical ideas clearly using appropriate diagrams and notations. [communicating, reasoning, big ideas about diagrams and notations] - Activity 1 2. Guide students to discover through investigation that the Pythagoras’ Theorem and its converse are equivalent statements. [big ideas about equivalence] - Activity 1 3. Provide students with opportunity to make sense of the trigonometric notations and appreciate that trigonometric ratios reflect the proportional relationships between pairs of sides of right-angled triangles. [big ideas about notations and proportionality] - Activity 2'}, {'row': 74, 'text': 'Teachers should 1. highlight volume and surface area as measures of the space and boundary of 3D solids. [big ideas about measures] 2. get students to observe and talk about the connections between the formulae for finding volume of a pyramid and its related prism, cone and its related cylinder, sphere and its related cone. [reasoning and communicating] Activity 1 3. strengthen students’ spatial visualisation by asking them to manipulate, examine and sketch the different perspectives of real-world objects. [big ideas about diagrams, visualising space, appreciation, motivation] Activity 2 4. create opportunities for students to use what they have learnt in mensuration to solve problems in which real-world objects are modelled using geometrical diagrams of basic solids. [big ideas about models and diagrams, appreciation, motivation] Activity 3 5. guide students to realise that surface area and volume of comp'}, {'row': 76, 'text': 'Teachers should 1. use everyday contexts to highlight probability as a measure of chance, the notations used to express this measure and its applications. [big ideas about measures and notations, applying and appreciation] Activity 1 2. discuss the experimental and theoretical approaches to finding probability and highlight the theoretical approach as a means to model real-life situations. [big ideas about models, reasoning, communicating] Activity 2'}, {'row': 78, 'text': 'Teachers should 1. strengthen students’ understanding of the features of dot diagrams, histograms and stem-and-leaf diagrams through opportunities to construct, interpret and discuss about these representations. [big ideas about diagrams, reasoning and communicating, metacognition] Activity 1 2. highlight mean, mode and median as measures of central tendency (centre) of a data set that can be used for analysis and comparison of data sets. [big ideas about measures] Activity 2'}, {'row': 80, 'text': '3. use appropriate problemsto create opportunities for students to reason, communicate and reflect on how different data values (including extreme values) affect statistical measures. [reasoning, communicating and metacognition] Activity 3'}], 'Sec 3 G3': [{'row': 86, 'text': 'Learning Experiences • Visualise and explore the symmetric properties of circles, e.g. “the perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre” using paper folding. [P/A] • Investigate the properties of circles with ICT and use geometrical terms correctly for effective communication. [P]'}, {'row': 90, 'text': '1. Appreciate that set notations are conventions used to describe and communicate information more concisely. [big ideas about notations, communicating] Activity 1 2. Appreciate that Venn diagrams are useful in representing information and relationships between elements of different sets in a compact and visual manner so as to facilitate interpretation of relationships. [big ideas about diagrams and notations] Activity 2 3. 𝑛(𝐴) is a measure of the number of elements in the set A and able to discuss the connections between this measure and the concepts of intersection and union sets. [big ideas about measures, communication and reasoning] Activity 3'}, {'row': 92, 'text': '1. Highlight how possibility diagrams and tree diagrams allow us to organise and present information in such a way so as to facilitate the computation of probabilities in a given event. [big ideas about diagrams, metacognition] 2. Discuss the use of probabilities of mutually exclusive versus non-mutually exclusive and independent versus dependent events as measures to model real-life situations. [big ideas about models, reasoning, communicating]'}, {'row': 95, 'text': '1. Highlight range, interquartile range and standard deviation as measures of spread of a data set that can be used for analysis and comparison of data sets through a constructivist approach. [big ideas about measures, reasoning and communicating] 2. Discuss with students the effects on measures of spread of adding the same value to every data point in a data set. [big ideas about invariance] Activity 2 3. Strengthen students’ understanding of the features of statistical diagrams (including cumulative frequency curves and box-and-whisker plots) through opportunities to discuss about these representations and their usefulness for visualising spread. [big ideas about diagrams, reasoning and communicating, metacognition] Activity 3'}, {'row': 98, 'text': '1. Appreciate the usefulness of matrices in real-life. [appreciation] Activity 1 2. Construct their understanding about matrices, and consolidate and communicate mathematical ideas clearly using appropriate notations. [communicating, big ideas about notations] Activity 2 3. Discover that addition of matrices is commutative. [big ideas about invariance] Activity 2 4. Appreciate scalar multiplication of matrices through a problem-posing learning experience. [appreciation] Activity 2 5. Understand the conventions of matrix multiplication and appreciate its applications involving complex or multiple iterations. [big ideas about notations, applying] Activity 3'}, {'row': 101, 'text': '1. Use authentic contexts to help students appreciate how vectors are used to measure physical qualities involving both magnitude and directions. [big ideas about measures, applying, appreciation] Activity 1 2. Highlight the use and interpretation of vector diagrams and vector notations to help students understand vector concepts, in particular vector addition and subtraction, and facilitate problem solving. [big ideas about diagrams and notations] Activity 2 3. Highlight the commutative property of vector addition and link it to the commutative property for addition of real numbers and matrices. [big ideas about invariance] Activity 2'}], 'Sec 1 G1': [{'row': 108, 'text': 'Identify and discuss numbers in real life (e.g. temperatures below 0 degree Cel., height below sea level, money, fractions in recipes, population, etc.). Estimate quantities (numbers and measures) in a variety of contexts, compare the estimates and discuss the estimates strategies."'}, {'row': 114, 'text': 'Make sense of addition, subtraction and multiplication involving negative integers using algebra discs or other concrete approaches and develop proficiency in the 4 operations of integers.'}, {'row': 117, 'text': 'Identify and discuss the use of ratios and proportions in real life (e.g. recipes and golden ratio, etc.). Represent ratios as fractions and make connections amongst these forms, e.g. the ratio of A to B is 2: 3 can be represented as: The ratio 2:3 means “2 units to 3 units”, “A is 2/3 of B”, or “B is 3/2 of A”.'}, {'row': 123, 'text': 'Discuss the use of percentage in the examples collected from newspaper, bills, receipts and do computation involving discounts, sales, GST, income tax, service charge. Make connections between percentages and fractions/decimals, e.g. “25% of a quantity = 1/4 of the quantity = 0.25 of a quantity”.'}, {'row': 132, 'text': 'Identify and classify angles found from real-life and use the correct term to describe them.'}, {'row': 135, 'text': 'Compare if expressions like 2n, 2+n and n2 are equivalent by using spreadsheets. Make sense of and interpret linear expressions with integral coefficients, e.g. 4𝑥 −3𝑦 by using algebra discs.'}, {'row': 139, 'text': 'Explore symmetric properties (line and rotational symmetry) of plane figures in real life including letters and numbers.'}, {'row': 141, 'text': 'Identify the height corresponding to any given base of a triangle or quadrilateral in order to apply the formula for area correctly. Make connection between the circumference of a circle and its diameter eg. Drawing several circles and find that for every circle, 𝑐𝑖𝑟𝑐𝑢𝑚𝑓𝑒𝑟𝑒𝑛𝑐𝑒 /𝑑𝑖𝑎𝑚𝑒𝑡𝑒𝑟 = 𝑐𝑜𝑛𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑛𝑡 Discover the formula for area of a circle is related to area of rectangle by observing the sectors of the circle being rearranged to a shape which resembles a rectangle. Make connection between the area of a parallelogram and that of a rectangle . Make connection between the area of trapezium and area of 2 triangles with the same height or make connection between the area of trapezium and that of a parallelogram.'}, {'row': 148, 'text': 'Visualise and draw the nets of cubes and cuboids for the calculation of surface area. Make connections between areas of squares in cm2 and m2 and between volumes of cubes in cm3 and m3 using a cube-cm manipulative and a cubic-metre structure'}, {'row': 153, 'text': 'Construct tables, bar graphs, pictograms, line graphs and pie charts to represent given data in order to make sense of the statistical diagrams and their features. Solve a problem statistically by working collaboratively on a task that involve: \uf0b7 posing a question that can be answered with real life data \uf0b7 collecting and classifying data \uf0b7 representing data using an appropriate statistical representation (including the use of software) \uf0b7 analysing data \uf0b7 interpreting the results Compare various statistical representations and justify why a particular representation is more suitable than others for a given situation.'}], 'Sec 2 G1': [{'row': 159, 'text': '- identify the properties of triangles, special quadrilaterals, perpendicular bisectors of line segments and angle bisectors - Construct triangles, Perpendicular Bisectors of Line Segments, Angle Bisectors and Quadrilaterals using ruler, protractor and compass'}, {'row': 160, 'text': '- understand what these words meant: - expression - coefficient - constant term - like / unlike terms - add and subtract linear expressions with multiple terms, variable and/or constants - add, subtract and simplify linear expression involving brackets and fractions with numerical denominators'}, {'row': 161, 'text': "- understand what the term equation mean - solve linear equations in one variable with/without brackets with the understanding that solving linear equation is about 'balancing' the equation - check if the answer after solving a linear equation is correct - formulate a linear equation in one variable to solve problems"}, {'row': 162, 'text': '- Understand what are cartesian coordinates in two dimensions - State, plot and label coordinates of given points - Understand what these words meant: - Coordinates - x- coordinate, y-coordinate - Origin - Cartesian coordinate system - Cartesian Plane - function, input, output - linear graph - y-intercept - Linear functions are in the form of 𝑦=𝑎𝑥+𝑏 - Linear functions can be represented as straight lines on a cartesian plane - Draw a straight line on graph given the linear function - Find unknown values from the graph, given certain conditions - understand that steepness of a linear graph can be represented by the term gradient as vertical change against horizontal change - understand the relationship between positive/negative value of gradient and slope going upwards/downwards - Calculate the gradient of any linear graph - Relate linear function 𝑦=𝑎𝑥+𝑏 to gradient and y-intercept - Form the linear function 𝑦=𝑎𝑥+𝑏 using cartesian plane - Relate horizontal lines as linear functions y = a, where a = y - intercept - Relate vertical lines as linear functions x = a, where a = x - intercept - Solve questions related to graphs in practical situation'}, {'row': 163, 'text': '- understand that ratio is an example of direct proportion - Share examples of direct and inverse proportion - Find specific variable in a context involving direct/inverse proportion - Understand that direct/inverse proportion can be represented with a straight line graph that passes through the origin - Understand that straight line graphs that does not pass through the origin shows that the 2 quantities are not proportional - Solve problems involving direct and inverse proportion'}, {'row': 164, 'text': '- Understand that rate is an example of direct/inverse proportion - Convert units of rate - Find rates and average rates including speed and average speed - Understand the usage of rate'}, {'row': 165, 'text': '- Understand • what is money exchange and why are there money exchange • what is simple interest • what are hire purchase and instalments and why do we have instalments • what are the types of taxations in Spore and why do we pay tax e.g. GST, Income tax, road tax, property tax - Understand how • money exchange works • instalments and hire purchase works • people are taxed in Singapore • Utilities bills are calculated - Apply skills in percentages, ratio and proportion in practical situations such as money exchange, simple interest, hire purchase and instalments, taxation and utilities bills'}, {'row': 167, 'text': '- understand what are congruent figures - understand that similar triangles and quadrilaterals have: • corresponding angles that are equal • corresponding sides that are proportional - finding unknown angles / sides of congruent and similar figures'}, {'row': 168, 'text': "- understand what is the hypothenuse - Know the Pythagoras' Theorem - Apply the Pythagoras' Theorem to right-angled triangles to find unknown sides - Use Pythagoras' Theorem to determine whether a triangle is a right-angled triangle given the lengths of 3 sides"}, {'row': 169, 'text': '- analysis and interpretation of: • dot diagrams • histograms with equal class intervals - understand purposes and uses, advantages and disadvantages of the above different forms of statistical representations - understand the purposes and uses of mean, mode and median - calculate the mean, mode and median for a set of ungrouped data'}, {'row': 170, 'text': '- Understand terms such as - chance - outcome - event - random - probability - equally likely - fair - sure / impossible event - list possible outcomes in a chance situation - understand probabilty as a measure of chance of an event - understanding what are sure and impossible events and their probabilities - probability of an event not occuring - Finding probability from given data'}, {'row': 172, 'text': '- Visualise and draw the nets of prisms and cylinders for the calculation of surface area. - Calculate volume and surface area of prism and cylinder - Conversion between cm square and meter square, and between cm cube and meter cube - Calculate volume and surgace area of composite solids"'}], 'Sec 3 G1': [{'row': 177, 'text': '- write ordinary numbers in standard form - write numbers in standard form as ordinary numbers - evaluate expressions involving numbers in standard form'}, {'row': 178, 'text': '- Understand the use of map in relation to map dimentions to actual dimensions - Calcualte the actual length based on the scale given in map - Compute the area scale based on the length scale - Calculate the actual area based on the area scale'}, {'row': 179, 'text': '- expand the product of two linear expressions and simplify - multiply and divide algebraic fractions - complete a number and pattern sequence - write the expression for the nth term of a number sequence - use the expression for the nth term of a sequence'}, {'row': 181, 'text': '- factorise linear expressions with numnerical common factors - factorise quadratic expressions (use of multplication frame) - rewrite formula with given subject - evaluate unknown quantity in a given formula'}, {'row': 183, 'text': '- calculate surface area and volumes of regular pyramids, cones and spheres - calculate unknown dimensiosn given surface area and volume - unit conversion'}, {'row': 184, 'text': '- derive cumulative frequency table - locate and find the values of the lower quartile, upper quartile and percentiles - calculate interquartile range - calculate probability'}, {'row': 187, 'text': '- expression given equation in the form y = mx + c, to find teh gradient and y-intercept of a straight line - plot a linear graph'}, {'row': 189, 'text': '- formulate a pair of simultaneous linear equations in two variables to solve problems - solve simultaneous linear equations'}], 'Sec 4 G1': [{'row': 194, 'text': '7.4. solving simple fractional equations that can be reduced to linear equations,'}, {'row': 195, 'text': '6.6. quadratic functions (𝑦=𝑎𝑥2+𝑏𝑥+𝑐) 6.7. graphs of quadratic functions and their properties - positive or negative coefficient of 𝑥2 - maximum and minimum points - symmetry'}, {'row': 198, 'text': '3.3. use of trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine and tangent) of acute angles to calculate unknown sides and angles in right-angled triangles (including problems involving angles of elevation and depression)'}], 'Sec 1 G2': [{'row': 203, 'text': 'Highlight that the prime factorisation of a number (including index notation) is an equivalent form which allows us to study the characteristics of that number. [big ideas about equivalence and notation] Activity 1 Provide mathematical statements where students need to reason and justify by applying their understanding of factors, multiples and prime factorisation. [applying, communication and reasoning] Activity 1'}, {'row': 206, 'text': 'Highlight that, given any sequence of numbers (applies to all real numbers, including negatives, and later extending to algebra), re-arranging the sequence does not change its sum or product. [big ideas about invariance] Activity 1 Extend understanding with the number line to real numbers (including negatives), appreciate it as a representation that allows us to order and compare numbers, and understand that there are infinitely many values between two points on the number line. [big ideas about diagrams] Activity 2'}, {'row': 208, 'text': 'Use real-world contexts to help students appreciate the use of significant figures and how rounding off to different degrees of accuracy can have an impact on subsequent computation. [applying, appreciation] Activity 3'}, {'row': 210, 'text': '1. Verbalise the structures in algebraic expressions and the manipulations involved in simplifying them. This can be done using algebra discs to help students visualise. [communicating] Activity 1 Part A 2. Abstract the general properties in algebraic manipulations. [big ideas about equivalence] Activity 1 Part B 3. Create and manipulate their own algebraic expressions, and then to reflect on their approaches [interest, reasoning and metacognition] Activity 2'}, {'row': 211, 'text': 'Role-model and then get students to verbalise the structures in algebraic expressions and the manipulations involved in simplifying them. This can be done using algebra discs to help students visualise. [communicating] Activity 1 Part A 2. Help students to abstract the general properties in algebraic manipulations. [big ideas about equivalence] Activity 1 Part B'}, {'row': 216, 'text': '1. Provide examples and explain using the notion of variable and unknown to help students distinguish the difference between algebraic expressions and equations [concept of equation] Activity 1 2. Highlight the interpretation of the equal sign in an equation, and the meaning of “solving an equation”, “solution of an equation” and “equivalent equations” [concept of equation] Activity 1 3. Use the metaphor of algebra discs on a balance to help students understand that the algorithm of solving a linear equation is premised on the idea of equivalence [big idea of equivalence] Activity 2 4. Compare and critique different methods of solving an equation and reflect on one’s own approach and answer [reasoning and metacognition] Activity 3, Question 1 5. Appreciate and ease into the formulation of linear equations and use the algebraic method to solve word problems [transition from the model method] Activity 4'}, {'row': 219, 'text': 'Use geometric patterns to discuss how we can describe and formulate the general term of a sequence in different ways and hence leverage the opportunity to talk about equivalence expressions. [reasoning, big ideas about equivalence] Activity 3'}, {'row': 221, 'text': '1. Highlight the big ideas about proportionality in percentages, i.e. if x% is related to y, 1% is related to y/x, the constant of proportionality, and y/x can be used to find other percentages. [big ideas about proportionality] 2. Highlight that a number can be represented as a fraction, decimal and percentage with the same value, and that we use the equivalent form which is best for analysis, interpretation or comparison, depending on the context. [big ideas about equivalence] 3. Provide opportunities to reason and communicate when using percentages for comparison and when discussing percentage increase or decrease relative to the initial values. To distinguish between absolute comparison and relative comparison [reasoning and communicating; addressing misconception] Activity 1 & 2 4. Reflect on their strategies when solving problems involving percentages [applying and metacognition] Activity 3'}, {'row': 223, 'text': '1. Verbalise the relationship between two quantities using ratios [reasoning and communicating] Activity 1 2. Solve problems that allow students to distinguish between absolute comparison and relative comparison [addressing misconception] Activity 2 and generate equivalent ratios [big ideas about equivalence] Activity 3 3. Highlight the connections between ratios, fractions and decimals, e.g. given a : b = 1 : 4, we can also said that a is 1/4 of b or a is 1/5 of the whole (assuming as sum of a and b), or b is 0.8 of the whole [big ideas about equivalence] 4. Bring out the idea of proportionality and that ratio expresses proportional relationship between quantities and hence allow one quantity to be computed from the other based on multiplication reasoning [big ideas about proportionality] Activity 4 5. Solve problems involving ratios in different contexts and find ‘the point of comparison’, and evaluate and reflect on their approaches [applying, reasoning and metacognition] Activity 5 & 6'}, {'row': 224, 'text': '1. Highlight that a rate is a measure combining measures of two attributes and the unit used is derived from the units of the two measures. [big ideas about measures] Activity 1 2. Use meaningful and interesting contexts to get students to talk about the difference between speed and average speed. [interest/appreciation/motivation, reasoning and communicating] Activity 2 & 3 3. Bring out the idea of proportionality in different contexts involving (constant) rates and highlight that there is a constant (of proportionality) that relates the two attributes. [big ideas about proportionality] Activity 4 4. Use rates to solve problems, and reflect on their strategies [applying and metacognition] Activity 5'}, {'row': 228, 'text': '1. Highlight that a rate is a measure combining measures of two attributes and the unit used is derived from the units of the two measures. [big ideas about measures] Activity 1 2. Use meaningful and interesting contexts to get students to talk about the difference between speed and average speed. [interest/appreciation/motivation, reasoning and communicating] Activity 2 & 3 3. Bring out the idea of proportionality in different contexts involving (constant) rates and highlight that there is a constant (of proportionality) that relates the two attributes. [big ideas about proportionality] Activity 4 4. Use rates to solve problems, and reflect on their strategies [applying and metacognition] Activity 5'}, {'row': 229, 'text': '1. Highlight that an angle is a measure of the amount of rotation between two lines so that it can be analysed and compared. [big ideas about measures] Activity 1 2. Strengthen students’ spatial visualisation by exposing them to a variety of geometrical drawings in different orientations and hands-on experiences such as paper-folding. [visualising space] Activity 2 3. Help students establish the properties of geometrical objects through inductive or deductive reasoning. [big ideas about invariance] Activity 3'}, {'row': 232, 'text': '1. Highlight that area and perimeter are measures of the space and boundary of 2D shapes while volume and surface area are measures of the space and boundary of 3D solids. [big ideas about measures] Activity 1 2. Create opportunities for students to use what they have learnt in mensuration to solve problems in which real-world objects are modelled using basic shapes and solids [big ideas about models, appreciation, motivation] Activity 1 3. Get students to observe and talk about the connections between the formulae for finding area and volume of related shapes and slides. [reasoning and communicating] Activity 2 4. Guide students to realise that composing and decomposing 2D shapes and 3D solids conserves their area and volume. [visualising space] Activity 2 5. Strengthen students’ spatial visualisation by asking them to manipulate, examine and sketch the different perspectives of real-world objects. [big ideas about diagrams, visualising space, appreciation, motivation] Activity 3'}, {'row': 236, 'text': '1. Pose and solve problems that require collecting, organising, representing, analysing and interpreting data. [applying, communicating, metacognition, appreciation and motivation] Activity 1 2. Highlight the importance of proportionality in statistical diagrams [big ideas about proportionality, big Ideas about diagrams] Activity 2'}], 'Sec 2 G2': [{'row': 240, 'text': 'Compare the methods of solving a linear inequality and the corresponding linear equation, and their solutions.'}, {'row': 243, 'text': '(a) Represent points using 2D Cartesian coordinates. (b) Make sense of the concept of function as “only one output for every input” and represent the function in verbal, tabular, graphical and algebraic forms by using a function machine to generate input and output values. (c) Explore how the graph changes when either a or b varies using ICT.'}, {'row': 246, 'text': 'Draw the graph of ax by c + = (a straight line), check that the coordinates of a point on the straight line satisfy the equation, and explain why the solution of a pair of simultaneous linear equations is the point of intersection of two straight lines, using ICT'}, {'row': 249, 'text': 'Explain the following processes, using algebra discs and the multiplication frame: i. expanding the product of two linear expressions of the form px q + , where p and q are integers, to obtain a quadratic expression of the form 𝑎𝑥2 +𝑏𝑥 +𝑐; ii. factorising quadratic expressions, highlighting expansion and factorisation as reverse processes of each other.'}, {'row': 259, 'text': 'Discuss examples of direct and inverse proportion and explain the concepts using tables, equations and graphs.'}, {'row': 261, 'text': 'Teachers should: 1. Guide students to use relevant features to describe or classify the figures, then make conjectures about the properties of these figures based on their observations and intuitions. [reasoning and communicating] Activity 1 2. Help students establish the properties of polygons by exploring with multiple examples, using drawings or concrete manipulatives to aid in visualisation and writing simple mathematical proofs. [big ideas about invariance, visualising space, reasoning and communicating] Activity 2 & 3 3. Let students explore and talk about the symmetric properties of regular polygons using concrete manipulatives or dynamic geometry software. [visualising space, reasoning and communicating] Activity 4'}, {'row': 266, 'text': '1) Get students to discuss ideas about similar figures or objects. [reasoning and communicating] 2) Provide opportunity for students to articulate the proportional relationship in a pair of similar figures and a family of similar figures. [big ideas about proportionality & notations, metacognition] 3) Create opportunity for students to appreciate the application of the concept of similarity in daily life. [applying]'}, {'row': 268, 'text': '1) students construct their understanding about Pythagoras’ Theorem, and consolidate and communicate mathematical ideas clearly using appropriate diagrams and notations. [communicating, reasoning, big ideas about diagrams and notations] 2) Students to discover through investigation that the Pythagoras’ Theorem and its converse are equivalent statements. [big ideas about equivalence] 3) Provide students with opportunity to make sense of the trigonometric notations and appreciate that trigonometric ratios reflect the proportional relationships between pairs of sides of right-angled triangles. [big ideas about notations and proportionality]'}, {'row': 269, 'text': 'Teachers should 1. highlight volume and surface area as measures of the space and boundary of 3D solids. [big ideas about measures] 2. get students to observe and talk about the connections between the formulae for finding volume of a pyramid and its related prism, cone and its related cylinder, sphere and its related cone. [reasoning and communicating] Activity 1 3. strengthen students’ spatial visualisation by asking them to manipulate, examine and sketch the different perspectives of real-world objects. [big ideas about diagrams, visualising space, appreciation, motivation] Activity 2 4. create opportunities for students to use what they have learnt in mensuration to solve problems in which real-world objects are modelled using geometrical diagrams of basic solids. [big ideas about models and diagrams, appreciation, motivation] Activity 3 5. guide students to realise that surface area and volume of comp'}, {'row': 272, 'text': 'Teaching Approaches Teachers should 1. use everyday contexts to highlight probability as a measure of chance, the notations used to express this measure and its applications. [big ideas about measures and notations, applying and appreciation] 2. discuss the experimental and theoretical approaches to finding probability and highlight the theoretical approach as a means to model real-life situations. [big ideas about models, reasoning, communicating]'}, {'row': 274, 'text': 'strengthen students’ understanding of the features of dot diagrams, histograms and stem- and-leaf diagrams through opportunities to construct, interpret and discuss about these representations. [big ideas about diagrams, reasoning and communicating, metacognition] Activity 1'}, {'row': 276, 'text': 'Teachers should 1. strengthen students’ understanding of the features of dot diagrams, histograms and stem_x0002_and-leaf diagrams through opportunities to construct, interpret and discuss about these representations. [big ideas about diagrams, reasoning and communicating, metacognition] Activity 1 2. highlight mean, mode and median as measures of central tendency (centre) of a data set that can be used for analysis and comparison of data sets. [big ideas about measures] Activity 2'}], 'Sec 3 G2': [{'row': 283, 'text': '5.14. factorisation of linear expressions of the form ax + bx + kay + kby 5.15. expansion of the product of algebraic expressions 5.16. changing the subject of a formula 5.17. finding the value of an unknown quantity in a given formula'}, {'row': 293, 'text': "Understand the general shape and properties of the graph of a quadratic function, recognizing it as a parabola. Identify key features of quadratic graphs, including the vertex, axis of symmetry, and direction of opening (upward or downward). Interpret the effects of changes in the coefficients of y=ax'2 +bx+c on the shape and position of the graph. Determine the maximum or minimum points of a quadratic function based on the vertex. Sketch the graph of a quadratic function by identifying intercepts, vertex, and symmetry."}, {'row': 296, 'text': "Use the graph of a quadratic function to find the solutions (roots) of a quadratic equation in one variable. Interpret the points where the graph intersects the x-axis as the solutions to the equation ax'2+bx+c=0. Understand the meaning of different types of roots (real and distinct, real and repeated, or no real roots) based on the graph. Estimate the solutions of a quadratic equation by reading the x-intercepts of the graph. Use a graphical approach to verify solutions found algebraically or estimate solutions when exact algebraic methods are complex."}, {'row': 298, 'text': 'See above'}, {'row': 300, 'text': 'See above'}, {'row': 301, 'text': '4.1 Positive Integer Indices and Laws of Indices Understand and apply the laws of indices for positive integer powers. Simplify expressions involving multiplication and division of terms with integer indices. 4.2 Zero and Negative Integer Indices Understand the concept of zero as an exponent. Apply the laws of indices to simplify expressions with zero and negative indices. 4.3 Fractional Indices Interpret and simplify expressions involving fractional indices. Relate fractional indices to roots (e.g., square roots, cube roots). 4.4 Solving Equations Involving Indices Solve simple equations that include terms with integer and fractional indices. 4.5 Standard Form Use standard form (scientific notation) to express large and small numbers. Perform operations with numbers in standard form. 4.6 Application of Indices to Compound Interest Apply the concept of indices in calculating compound interest. Understand and solve problems related to exponential growth in financial contexts.'}], 'Sec 4 G2': [{'row': 311, 'text': '• Match and justify sketches of graphs with their respective functions, plot graphs • Discover, through investigation, the properties of power and exponential functions using the algebraic and graphical representations, and appreciate big ideas about functions and diagrams. • Appreciate how functions can be used as models of real-world situations and phenomena, and to identify assumptions and limitations.'}, {'row': 313, 'text': '• Find gradient of curves and interpret graphs'}, {'row': 314, 'text': '• visualise and communicate the use of mathematical statements such as ‘angle of depression of X from Y’ and ‘bearings of X from Y’, and highlight the convention used in describing bearings.'}, {'row': 318, 'text': '• communicate their ideas on mensuration using mathematical language. • identify the proportional relationships between quantities (radius, arc length, sector angle, area of sector), and articulate their understanding on proportionality.'}, {'row': 319, 'text': '• communicate their ideas on mensuration using mathematical language. • identify the proportional relationships between quantities (radius, arc length, sector angle, area of sector), and articulate their understanding on proportionality.'}, {'row': 320, 'text': '• visualise and estimate angles in radians so that they can check the reasonableness of their answers when finding angles on diagrams in radians.'}, {'row': 321, 'text': '• Highlight range, interquartile range and standard deviation as measures of spread of a data set that can be used for analysis and comparison of data sets through a constructivist approach. • Understand the effects on measures of spread of adding the same value to every data point in a data set. • Understanding of the features of statistical diagrams (including cumulative frequency curves and box-and-whisker plots) and their usefulness for visualising spread.'}, {'row': 323, 'text': 'See above'}, {'row': 324, 'text': 'See above'}, {'row': 325, 'text': '• Understand how possibility diagrams and tree diagrams allow us to organise and present information in such a way so as to facilitate the computation of probabilities in a given event. • Understand the use of probabilities of mutually exclusive versus non-mutually exclusive and independent versus dependent events as measures to model real-life situations.'}, {'row': 326, 'text': 'See above'}, {'row': 328, 'text': '• discover various circle properties and appreciate the invariance of these properties. • explain their approaches when solving problems involving circle properties and reflect on the efficiency of various approaches, while finding opportune moments to address common misconceptions involving circle properties • engage in deductive reasoning and appreciate the inter- connectedness of various circle properties.'}]}
